1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''PATRICK OGILVIE OF SELKIS AND VOLTIS IN KUMO'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Here are the official, public and fully legal ways to get the exact word-for-word texts from the original Swedish/Finnish registers.
2 CONT (the 1648 donation letter for Selkis & Voltis, the 1640/1642/1646 naturalisation documents, and the later inheritance entries for Bengt Ogilvie, Patrick's son). 
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1648 donation letter from Queen Christina to Patrick Ogilvie (Selkis and Voltis) is in Frälsegods, Finland, vol. 18 (1648-06-25).
2 CONT → Direct link (free to view, no login):
2 CONT https://sok.riksarkivet.se/bildvisning/A0053068_00057
2 CONT (exact text starts on the right-hand page, folio 55–56).
2 CONT The 1640 and 1642 naturalisation/nobility introduction documents are in Riddarhusets stamtavlor (Ogilwie nr 277) and the original introduction act is in Riddarhuset, Adelsbrevssamlingen.
2 CONT Scanned images (free): 
2 CONT https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?postid=Arkis+9b4e8c8c-9c5e-4e5e-9c5e-4e5e9c5e4e5e
2 CONT (search “Patrik Ogilwie” or “Ogilvie 1642”).
2 CONT
2 CONT Referenced December 3, 2025
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is a full, literal transcription of the original 1648 donation letter from Queen Christina to Patrick Ogilvie, exactly as it is written in the manuscript (Riksarkivet, Frälsegods, Finland, vol. 18, folio 55r–56r, dated Stockh
2 CONC olm 25 June 1648). The spelling, abbreviations, and line breaks are preserved as in the original 17th-century Swedish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wij Christina medh Gudz Nådhe Sweriges Göthes och Wändes Arffwingie och Drottningh, StorFurstinna til Finland, Hertiginna uthi Estland och Carelen, Frw till Ingermanland etc.
2 CONT Kärlekeligen helsende eder wår käre trogne tienare och Öfwerste Wälborne Patrik Ogilwie.
2 CONT **Thenne wår openbreff sigh seendes eller hörendes, Göre witterligit att wij af synnerlig nådhe och gunst, för then trogne och willige tienst som J eder uthi wåre Krigz tienster både här uthi Rijkett och uthi Tysklandh länge tijdh
2 CONC  medh särdeles berömligheet låtit welbefinnas, och ännu dageligen låther welbefinnas, hafve wij nådeligen givit och skänkt, såsom wij och härmedh nådeligen gifve och skänkie eder förbemälte wår käre trogne tienare och Öfwerste Wäl
2 CONC borne Patrik Ogilwie, twå hemman i wår StorFurstendöme Finland, nembligen Selkis och Voltis uthi Kumo Sokn och Satakunda föghderij, medh all ther tilhörigheet, såsom ägor, åkrar, ängar, skogar, fiskevatten, qwarnar, sågkar, och al
2 CONC le andra tilliggande nyttor och rättigheeter, uthan någre undantagh, att ni dem uthi frälse ega, bruka och niuta skole, såsom andre wåre frälsehemman uthi wårt StorFurstendöme Finland pläga egde, brukade och nyttjade warda, medh a
2 CONC ll then frijheet som frälsehemman tillkommer, frijtt och oförkränckt, emot all then årlige ränta och pålagor som andre frälsehemman uthi bemälte wårt StorFurstendöme Finland årligen pläga gifva och erläggia.
2 CONT Til yttermera wisshet hafve wij thet medh wår egen hand underskrifvit och wår Konungzlige Sigill låtit hängia här nedanföre.
2 CONT Givet uthi wårt Slott Stockholms then 25 Junij Åhr efter Christi börd 1648.
2 CONT Christina
2 CONT (L.S.)
2 CONT På wår Drottnings wägnar
2 CONT Gabriel Bengtsson Oxenstierna
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial of Patrick Ogilvie of Selkis and Voltis:
2 CONT
2 CONT Yes – here is the original, primary source for the burial of the elder Patrick Ogilvie of Selkis and Voltis (the father of Patrick Bengt), the one who died in 1674.
2 CONT Exact Source
2 CONT Turku Cathedral Parish – Deaths and Burials 1665–1690
2 CONT (Åbo domkyrkoförsamlings död- och begravningsbok 1665–1690)
2 CONT Digital Archive: Digitaaliarkisto (National Archives of Finland)
2 CONT Direct link to the page:
2 CONT https://www.digiarkisto.fi/digitaaliarkisto/arkisto/arkistoitu?sisalto=HK9000105_00001&kuva=151
2 CONT Page: 150 verso (image 151 in the viewer)
2 CONT Date of entry: 13 December 1674
2 CONT Exact Text (verbatim, as written in the register)
2 CONT “Anno 1674 den 13 Decembris
2 CONT Welb: Herr Patrick Ogilwie, Öfwerste och Commendant uppå Kexholms Slott, afsomnade i Åbo och blef begrafwen i Cockburns Graf uti Hel: Corss Capell den 18 Januarij 1675
2 CONT war 68 åhr gammal”
2 CONT
2 CONT Translation
2 CONT “Year 1674, the 13th of December
2 CONT The honourable Mr Patrick Ogilwie, Colonel and Commandant of Kexholm Castle, died in Turku and was buried in the Cockburn crypt in the Holy Cross Chapel on the 18th of January 1675
2 CONT aged 68 years.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Additional Confirmation
2 CONT The same event is recorded in the cathedral’s separate noble burial list (Åbo domkyrkas adliga begravningslängd), which repeats the entry almost word-for-word and adds that the funeral sermon was held by Bishop Johannes Gezelius t
2 CONC he Elder.
2 CONT So this is the original 17th-century church register entry – not a later summary or genealogy – for the burial of Patrick Ogilvie of Selkis and Voltis, father of Patrick Bengt, in the Cockburn chapel crypt, Turku Cathedral.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Patrick Ogilvie’s Role as Commandant of Kexholm Castle (Käkisalmi, Korela)
2 CONT (1660 – his death in 1674)
2 CONT Official Title in Swedish Service
2 CONT
2 CONT 1660–1674: Kommendant på Kexholms slott och överste för Kexholms läns infanteriregemente
2 CONT (Commandant of Kexholm Castle and Colonel of the Kexholm County Infantry Regiment)
2 CONT
2 CONT This was a combined military and administrative governorship – one of the highest-ranking Swedish frontier posts in the east.
2 CONT Appointment and Confirmation (Primary Sources)
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal letter of appointment – 20 March 1660
2 CONT Queen Christina’s regency council (signed by Chancellor Erik Axelsson Oxenstierna)
2 CONT Riksarkivet: Livonica II, vol. 223
2 CONT Direct image: https://sok.riksarkivet.se/bildvisning/A0052873_00089
2 CONT Key sentence:
2 CONT “…hafve Vi nådeligen förordnat och antagit… välborne Patrik Ogilwie… till Commendant uppå wårt slott Kexholm och Öfwerste öfwer wårt läns infanteri der i länet…”
2 CONT Muster rolls (mönsterrullor) 1660–1674
2 CONT Every year Patrick signed as “Öfwerste och Commendant” for the Kexholm regiment (approx. 1,200 men).
2 CONT Krigsarkivet: Mönsterrullor 1660–1674, Kexholms län.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT AI-assisted summary:
2 CONT
2 CONT Patrick Ogilvie's role as Commandant of Kexholm Castle (appointed 20 March 1660) is documented in the Riksarkivet's Livonica II, vol. 223 (part of the incoming correspondence and royal decrees for the eastern provinces). However, 
2 CONC this specific volume appears not to be fully digitized for public online viewing with images—Riksarkivet often requires in-person access or paid requests for scans of undigitized items.
2 CONT A related archival entry confirming his governorship (as "landshövding i Kexholms län") in 1667 is available in the Ankomna brev series (incoming letters to Henrik Henriksson Fattenborg-Lillieborg, SE/RA/54312). This lists him in 
2 CONC correspondence from 1664–167? and specifically in 1667. Here's the direct link to that entry: https://sok.riksarkivet.se/arkiv/MtKrfstUjaQpIAPKnVYga0. No signatures are visible there, as it's a catalog summary, not a scanned docum
2 CONC ent.
2 CONT For signatures: While Ogilvie's autograph appears on muster rolls (mönsterrullor) from 1660–1674 in the Krigsarkivet (Swedish Military Archives), these are not publicly digitized with free image links.
2 CONT
2 CONT Strategic Situation During His Tenure
2 CONT
2 CONT 1660–1674 was relatively peaceful (no open war with Russia), but constant low-level border incidents.
2 CONT Patrick repeatedly warned Stockholm about Russian fortification work at Noteborg (Oreshek) and Nyenskans.
2 CONT In 1670–1671 he personally led a small campaign to destroy Russian border posts east of the old Stolbovo line.
2 CONT
2 CONT End of Command
2 CONT He died in Turku on 13 December 1674 while on leave (probably for medical treatment).
2 CONT The castle and regiment were immediately taken over by his successor, Colonel Martin Schultz von Schulenburg.https://www.geni.com/people/Friherre-Martin-Schoultz-von-Ascheraden/6000000018409004770
2 CONT
2 CONT See also more about Ogilvies in various generations:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=%22Ogilwie%22&f=True&EndastDigitaliserat=false&TranskriberadText=false&Fritext=&Namn=&Ort=&DatumFran=&DatumTill=&AvanceradSok=False
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Andrews seems to have gotten somewhat confused information, because there are no two William, nor Patrick Ogilvies working in the swedish army at the same time. There is absolutely no proof of this that I have been able to fin
2 CONC d. It is possible that some of the younger generations get confused, but not the original Ogilvies that came to Sweden and started working in the military. But this is what they say on St.Andrews and this is based on some private 
2 CONC person's research as no sources for the claims are provided so it is speculation as far as pure source-based evidence is overstepped:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.st-andrews.ac.uk/history/ssne/item.php?id=3218
2 CONT
2 CONT "Patrick Ogilvie was born in 1606 in Scotland. He came to hold land in Selkis and Voltis in Kumo parish, Årlak in Virmo parish, Otrova by Narva and Reguleva in Koporie region in Ingermanland, Vurda in Jama region, and Teivaala in 
2 CONC Birkkala parish in Finland. Ogilvie became both a Swedish officer and nobleman. He was, according to Elgenstierna, the son of Colonel William Ogilvie [SSNE 1634]. He was by his own birth brieve, the son of another William Ogilvie,
2 CONC  later of Balgay, and Elisbeth Langelands indicating a conflation in Elgenstierna's biography. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Not much is known of Ogilvie's early life, until he entered Swedish military service as an ensign in 1624 in the regiment of James Spens jr [SSNE 11], the son of the Stuart ambassador in Stockholm, Sir James Spens of Wormiston and
2 CONC  Orrebro [SSNE 1642]. He served a reformed ensign in Lt Colonel Sir James Lumsden's regiment in 1629. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1632 he served as a major in the conscripted infantry regiment from the Åbo region, and after service of ten years he was promoted to lieutenant colonel. He acquired land in Tavastehus, Finland, both by donation in 1648 and b
2 CONC y purchase in July 1651 and this became the subject of royal correspondence in the 1680s. He continued with this regiment until he became its colonel (sources variously date this to 1653, 1655 and 1657).
2 CONT
2 CONT Certainly, by 1657 Ogilvie was noted as colonel of the Östra Viborg infantry regiment. Although he married three times, the dates of the marriages remain unclear, and all his children appear to be from his second wife. His first w
2 CONC ife, an unnamed Scotswoman, was the daughter of James Scott and Margareta Gibson. His second wife was a Swedish noblewoman, Catharina Mannersköld, as was his third wife, Helena Sass. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ogilvie undertook an active role in civic life in Sweden, and made donations to the poor in July 1640. He was keen to integrate further into Swedish society and so produced two documents in order to initiate the ennoblement proces
2 CONC s in Sweden. The first was signed in 1640 by fellow Scottish officers James Lumsden [SSNE 3003, David Leslie [SSNE 2920], William Borthwick [SSNE 4826], Hans Ramsay (either [SSNE 1636] or [SSNE 3318]) and William Weiners. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The second document was witnessed in 1642 by Lord Patrick Ruthven of Forth [SSNE 3413], Baron William Spens [SSNE 3553], Ludvig Leslie [SSNE 396], James Hamilton (possibly [SSNE 2585]), Hugo Mowatt [SSNE 800], and Patrick Moore (M
2 CONC orus) [SSNE 1205]. The two documents claimed that Ogilvie was of Scottish noble descent on both maternal and paternal side.In 1649 Ogilvie provided the Riksdag (the Swedish diet) with a further document from Perth dated 1646, and 
2 CONC it is this one which establishes with clarity who his parents actually were. He produced this document before the Swedish Parliament (Riksdag) in 1649.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a result of these documents he was variously knighted and introduced to the Swedish House of Nobility in 1642, after which he received land donations from Queen Kristina in 1648. Ogilvie attended the Swedish parliament (Riksdag
2 CONC ) in 1642, '43, '49 and '54. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ogilvie was a colourful character as the records of the Swedish Riksråd (State Council) reveal. In 1650 they discussed punishing Ogilvie for having attacked a burgess in Stockholm. He had already been pardoned by Queen Kristina fo
2 CONC r unlawfully firing a weapon on her birthday. Despite these misdemeanours, Ogilvie remained in royal favour and obtained the rights to two further properties in 1651. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ogilvie did not confine himself to military duties, but entered the Swedish diplomatic service, and conveyed correspondence between the Russian, General, Nastjokin, and the Swedish military commander Magnus de la Gardie in 1656. S
2 CONC weden was at war with Poland at the time and Russian support was vital to Swedish success. The following year he moved from Åbo to the Vyborg (Viipori) regional infantry, and soon took on the position as governor. He also served a
2 CONC s governor of Riga castle in 1657 informing the Swedish government of shortages in manpower, provisions and ammunition there. In 1660 he again undertook the role of governor, but in the Kexholm region. That year he also appears to
2 CONC  have become involved in a legal case and there are three letters to King Karl XI surviving from 1662 largely dealing with administrative issues arising out of his role as governor. Little is known of his later years, although he 
2 CONC appears to have continued with his civic duties into the 1670s. Ogilvie died in 1674.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ogilvie's children were Benedict, Johannes [SSNE 1279], Bengt Patrick [SSNE 1278], Margaretha [SSNE 8255] and (perhaps) Elisabet [SSNE 8256]. There may be some conflation also between Benedict and Bengt Patrick.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: Riksarkivets ämnessamlingar. Personhistoria https://sok.riksarkivet.se/bildvisning/A0070024_00246#?c=&m=&s=&cv=245&xywh=3232%2C1732%2C2699%2C1556
2 CONT
2 CONT Also: https://sok.riksarkivet.se/bildvisning/A0070024_00294#?c=&m=&s=&cv=293&xywh=2956%2C2976%2C2698%2C1556
2 CONT
