2 CONT
2 CONT I have studied the latin text now properly and come to the conclusion that in the text that Patrick Ogilvies' MATERNAL GRANDMOTHER is a daughter of Scott de Balwearie.  This must mean that considering the approximate year in quest
2 CONC ion for Elizabeth's birth, ''The father of Elizabeth Langlands' mother must be William Scott de Balwearie X (the younger). His father's first name name is also William.'' Observe! Not all information related to these lines for exa
2 CONC mple on wikitree are correct. In this specific family's case for example I have noticed there being what I assume a pure mistake considering the birthyear  of Andrew(Andro in scots) in relation to when he presumably died, and it i
2 CONC s this "Andrew b. circa 1595 his heir died circa 1560 [1]" -which clearly is impossible. I believe he meant to write ca, 1660 as the death year for Andrew, William's brother. There are also faults with Patrick Ogilvie's Paternal s
2 CONC ide, where he should have a paternal grandmother called Besseta aka Elizabeth Monargund, there now stands a completely other person unknown to this line. I am assuming it is because the Monargund line is extremely elusive, but aft
2 CONC er years of searching and searching I have found some information about them too and there indeed has been a Bessie or Elspeth Monargund which appears in many different written forms as at the time names and many words hadn't yet 
2 CONC gotten a standardised writing form. (again scots' way of writing the same name and Bessie and Besseta are both nicknames for Elizabeth and Monargund appears nowadays as Monorgan which is a location near the city of modern Dundee, 
2 CONC but has appeared in the records even as Monorgund, Monorgounde etc.) But this, like said is on Patrick Ogilvie's paternal line. I am just notifying that some things on wikitree are not correct whilst others most probably are.
2 CONT Therefore I will add here also the wikitree link even if I came to the same  conclusion about Elisabeth Langlands' mother being the Scott of Balwearie by studying the text in original format, not a google translation which I mysel
2 CONC f added some years ago without realising that information got twisted in the translation and it became a mess. Even if the text is a bit hard to read, it indeed lists first Patrick Ogilvie's paternal ancestry, then it goes on to s
2 CONC ay "Likewise on his mother's side..." Then follows the list for Elizabeth Langland's parentage and their female lines since the male lines are obvious by the surnames. Not everything else is right about Patrick Ogilvie's ancestry 
2 CONC on wikitree, but Scott de Balwearie being his maternal grandmother's lineage is.
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Langlands-179
2 CONT
2 CONT >''Text and link to wikitree added on the 19th of November, 2024 by Maria Thidell, the 10th great granddaughter of Elizabeth Langlands through her son Patrick Ogilvie of Selkis and Voltis.''
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Information provided by Patrick Ogilvy (1606-1674) for his nobilization in Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT ..."Patricii Ogiluie Vice - Collonelli praedicti Nativitatis Legitimae et Ingenuitatis Testimonium fidele daremus, Praesertim cum omnibus pene (eller "sene"?) Nostratibus constaret Patricium Ogiluie Vice-Colonellum praedicutm legi
2 CONC time natum, baptizatum et educatum, Nec non ex generosis et ingenuis parentibus Sancto Matrimonii Vinculo coram veneranda Ecclesia junctis legittime procreatum fuisse, Patre nimirum qvondam Willielmo Ogiluie de Balgaÿ ex domu de I
2 CONC nchmartine et Matre quondam '''Elizabetha LangLands filia domini de Collace''' intra Vice-Comitatum praescriptum, Ac etiam ex parte Patris Auia Monorgund de eodem, Proavia Haÿ de Meginch, et Mater sui Avi Rosse de Cragie, Et simil
2 CONC iter ex parte Matris Auia Scot de Balwerie Mater suae Auiae Aito ne de eodem, Ac Mater sui Aui Wishert de Logie. Propterea Nobilissimus et Potentissimus Comes Joannes Comes de Perth Dominus Drummound et Stobhall, Nec non Nobilissi
2 CONC mus et Potentissimus Comes Joannes Comes de Kingorne, Dominus Lyon et Glames, Nec non Nobillissimus et Potentissimus Comes Jacobus Comes de "Finlatore" Dominus Deffurd, Nec non Nobilissimus Dominus Patricius dominus de Deffurd: Ne
2 CONC c non Honorabiles et Potentes domini Dominus Patricius Ogilui de Inchmartine Dominus Thomas Blaire de Balthyoke, Virigraues et generosi Coram Nobis pro dicto tribunali praesentes Juramento adacti et axaminati, de more elatis Brach
2 CONC üs expansis manibus et in coelum erectis digitis":.... 
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the witnesses mentioned in the latin text is Earl John Lyon the 2nd of Kinghorne, who also had done business with Elisabeth Langland's father Patrick Langlands as mentioned in the below excerpt:
2 CONT
2 CONT ..."John, second Earl of Kinghorn, and tenth Lord Glamis, born 13 August 1596. He was served heir in the lordship of Glamis, under a special dispensation from the King 31 March 1617. - On 4 April 1617, he purchased from Patrick Ki
2 CONC nnaird of Inchture, the two parts of the lands of Mylnehill and the lands of Longforgan called the Byreflats for 22,000 merks. - Brydestown he purchased in 1619 from Patrick Langlands, portioner of Collace; - the lands of Lenros a
2 CONC nd Aikers of Baky from John Lyon of Westhill of Glamis 27 February 1621; - the lands of Tullos and Craichie from William, Earl of Morton and John Lyon of Auldbar in 1621. - About the same time, he acquired the patronage of Roscobi
2 CONC e, Airlie and Kinghorn."...
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:  http://venitap.com/Genealogy/WebCards/ps46/ps46_017.html
2 CONT
2 CONT "Biography Elizabeth was born about 1570 daughter to Patrick Langlands and his first wife, a daughter of Ross of Craigie.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1591 she married as second wife to William Ogilvy of Balgay. They had issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew b. circa 1595 his heir died circa 1560 [1] Patrick b. 1606 emigrated and known as Patrik Ogilwie Swedish Knight No 277
2 CONT
2 CONT On 13 June 1596, a Bond by David Maxwell of Teilling, Hew Maxwell his son and apparent hair, and Elizabeth Butter, spouse of the said Hew, with consent of Robert and Hew Maxwell, brothers of the said David, as principals, and Stev
2 CONC en Currour, younger of Logymeigill, as cautioner, to infeft William Ogilvy in Balgay and Elizabeth Langlands, his spouse, in a quarter of the said David and Hew Maxwell's half lands of Polgavie. The bond was witnessed by George Ma
2 CONC xwell in Balgray, Andrew Black, burgess of Dundee, George Black in Polgavie, James Gray, brother of Thomas Gray of Buttergask. John Auchinleck and William Haitlie were the notaries. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Seven months later on 13 Jan 1597/8 Elizabeth Langlands spouse to William Ogilvie in Balgay, confirmed an Alienation made by the said William and her to Patrick Kinnaird in Inchmichael, of a quarter of the half lands of Polgavie w
2 CONC ith the pendicles and pertinents thereof occupied by George Andro disponed to them as above in fue ferme by David Maxwell of Tealing and Hew Maxwell his son and heir, lying in the barony of and sheriffdom of Perth. George Andro ac
2 CONC knowledged Patrick Kinnaird to be his master to the quarter part lands. [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Redemption was made on 3 June 1598 by Alexander Gairdner in the east haugh of Ruthven, and Isobel Ruthven, his spouse, in favour of George Kynnaird in Inscheture, of an annual rent of 25 bolls victual furth of a quarter of the hal
2 CONC f lands of Polgavie, wadset by William Ogilwye in Balgay, and Elizabeth Langlands, his spouse, to Patrick Kynnaird in Inchemichel, and by him translated to the said George Kynnaird Description: Witnesses: John Lindsay of Boghall, 
2 CONC Mr. George Ruthuen, Andrew Ogilvye, burgesses of Perth, James Gray, brother of Patrick Gray of Balegerno, Alexander Don and William Robertson, notaries. [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1599 17 July registered 31 Jan 1606. Contract was agreed between William Ogilvy in Balgay and Elizabeth Langlands his spouse and Andro Ogilvy their eldest son on one part, and William Broun in Clene and Margaret Jackson his spouse
2 CONC  on the other, who for 3,000 merks gave sasine to the Brouns of the lands of Pockmill. This was witnessed at Clene 17 July 1599 by Gilbert Gray in North Bandirran, Patrick Ramsay son to Peter Ramsay of Clashbenny, and James Jackso
2 CONC un of Waterne. [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources ↑ NRS RD1/116 ↑ Perth & Kinross Archives; Rossie Priory Papers MS100/1/613 ↑ Dundee City Archives; Dundee Head Court Book /20 ↑ Perth & Kinross Archives; Rossie Priory Papers MS100/1/623 ↑ NRS RD1/116 registered 31 Jan 160
2 CONC 6. see [1]" (copied from https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Langlands-1799
