1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == PATRICK LANGLANDS OF LITTLE BUTTERGASK elder ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''12 January 1586: Charter by which John Langlands of Little Buttergask confirms that he has sold (vendidit) his one quarter part of the lands of Collace, and one eighth part of the lands Brydingstoun, in the sheriffdoms of Fife 
2 CONC and Forfar. to Helen Hay, daughter of Peter Hay of Leys. His charter follows from a contract of marriage between his son and apparent heir, Patrick Langlands, and Helen Hay and her father, Patrick Hay of Leys. The land is to be he
2 CONC ld by Helen and her lawful male heirs by Patrick Langlands, whom failing by Patrick's nearest male heir whatsoever bearing the name and arms of Langlands. The land in question was occupied by Davind Langlands, father of John Langl
2 CONC ands of Little Buttergask, and his liferent interest was preserved by this charter. The charter of John Langlands of Little Buttergask was confirmed under the Great Seal of Scotland on 6 December 1591. [https://archive.org/details
2 CONC /registrummagnisi05scot/page/669/mode/1up RMS 1580-93: charter number 1973]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''20 February 1612: Charter under the Great Seal of Scotland by which James VI, King of Scots, confirms possession of one quarter part of the lands of Collace, and one quarter part of the lands of Little Buttergask, in the sherif
2 CONC fdom of Perth, and one quarter of the lands of Brydingstoune in the sheriffdom of Angus, to Patrick Langlands, portioner of Collace, younger, and his future wife Agnes Ogilvie. Patrick Langlands, the younger, is identified as the 
2 CONC son of Patrick Langlands, portioner of Collace, elder, and Agnes Ogilvie is identified as the lawful daughter of the deceased John Ogilvie of Duntrune. The lands in question were to be held by Patrick and Agnes in conjunct fee and
2 CONC  liferent, and their lawful male heirs, whom failing by Patrick's nearest heirs whatsoever. These lands had previously been resigned by Patrick Langlands, elder and younger, in order to obtain a confirmation under the Great Seal o
2 CONC f Scotland. Patrick Langlands, elder, reserved his own liferent of Brydingstoune. [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi07scot/page/230/mode/1up RMS 1609-20: charter number 622 on pp. 230-31]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Printed Evidence ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''[https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi05scot/page/n6/mode/1up Registrum Magni Sigilli Regum Scotorum. The Register of the Great Seal of Scotland, A.D. 1580-1593. Edited by John Maitland Thomson, MA., Advocate. (H. M. G
2 CONC eneral Register House, Edinburgh, 1888), 1155 pp. including indices and errata]'''
2 CONT # '''[https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi07scot/page/n6/mode/1up Registrum Magni Sigilli Regum Scotorum. The Register of the Great Seal of Scotland, A.D. 1609-1620. Edited by John Maitland Thomson, MA., Advocate. (H. M. G
2 CONC eneral Register House, Edinburgh, 1892), 1152 pp. including indices and errata]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Langlands-180 Biographical Summary from Wikitree] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Patrick was born about 1550, eldest son to John Langlands of Little Buttergask and Collace and his spouse Janet Scott. About 1570 he married a daughter of Ross of Craigie. They had issue: Patrick b. circa 1574 his heir Elizabet
2 CONC h b. circa 1570 mar. Willian Ogilvy of Balgay His wife died about 1585 and Patrick married secondly in January 1586/7 to Helen Hay daughter to John Hay of Leys. Her brother arranged the contract of marriage with John Langlands, fa
2 CONC ther of the groom providing her tocher of 840 pounds. [1] At Collace in 1594, Patrick agreed with his father to grant to his son Patrick a quarter of the lands of Collace which David Langlands grandfather to Patrick had occupied a
2 CONC nd a quarter of the lands of Little Buttergask which John, Patrick's father occupied along with a quarter of the lands of Brydiston. John Langlands and his wife Janet Scott retained liferent of Little Butterston and Brydiston whil
2 CONC e Patrick senior with his wife Helen Hay retained liferent of Collace. Patrick Hay of Leys, Helen's brother was principal witness to this contract. [2] Sources 1. RMS Vol. 5 1973 2 RMS Vol. vi 203. [copied from https://www.wikitre
2 CONC e.com/wiki/Langlands-180]'''
